:::.col-12 .col-md-8
### Fibre {.text-center}

Fibre (or roughage) is an `essential component` of food obtained `mainly from plants`. It `consists chiefly of cellulose` found in `plant cell walls`. 

Although roughage `passes` through the intestines `undigested` and `provides no direct nutrients` to the body, it plays a critical protective role against `constipation, heart disease, and diabetes`.
